Ged takes the waterskin in both hands and drinks, gulping, water running down his chin into the dust of his beard-stubble. He forces himself to stop, breathes hard, and hands it back more than half empty, his eyes clearing a little.

"Bram... I don't know where he is now, not for certain. He was behind me when we ran, still on his feet, still carrying his lamp. We'd gone too deep, Brakka — past the old seals, down where the rails stop and the drift narrows to a stair cut in the rock. That's the deep workings. Half an hour down from here at a stoop, if you're whole. More for me, now."

He folds his arms across himself, and his voice drops again. "There's a whole shift of the old dead laid out down there. They look like they just lay down and never got up — all of them, the crew that never came out, years and years back. We were fools. We went down among them, and the cold came up out of that place, and the flame of our lamps thinned to a little blue needle — and then they began to move. That's when Tobis bolted, and we ran. Olin's lamp went out first. Then Bram's. I kept mine burning, and I didn't look back."

"Walk?" He almost laughs, a dry sound. "Aye. I'll walk. I'll crawl on my hands and knees if I have to. But the lights go with us — every one we can carry, every stub and every lamp in this barricade. If we climb up into the dark with less than we need, they'll rise behind us, and there won't be any barricade between us and them."

He looks past you toward the bend, toward the way out. "And Bram — if he's one of them now, he'll be somewhere between us and the air. Or down there with the rest of them, waiting for the dark."

The ring of small flames around him gutters and steadies, a dozen little lights keeping the cold at bay. Ged waits, watching your face, ready to move the moment you give the word.


>>>>>> round 14, the player: "Then we gather every lamp and stub, Ged, and we go up together: you in the middle, my shield between you and whatever's behind, and Olin's lantern in your hand." I start helping him pack the little flames into a sack and pouches, topping up the lanterns with my spare oil. "Once you're out and safe with Hessa, I'll decide about going down for Bram. How are you on your feet right now?"

  [time: 5 minutes later: Day 1, 5:10:24 PM]

Ged pushes himself up with the pick for a staff, and for a moment he sways, one hand on the timber. Then he plants his feet and straightens, and there's a stubborn set to his jaw that a day's walk won't break, whatever three days in the dark have done to him.

"I'll stand, and I'll walk," he says. "My legs are weak as a kitten's and I'd sleep on the stone if you let me, but I'd walk through fire to see the sun again. Don't fret over me, Brakka. Fret over the dark behind us."

You set to work with him, gathering the little ring of flames — the candle-stubs into a pouch, the small lamps slung and clipped so that a half-dozen lights move with you, bobbing in a little constellation around the two of you. It's awkward, and Ged is slow and fumbling with it, his fingers cold, but piece by piece the barricade is stripped of its light and gathered onto the pair of you.

When you look to top up the lanterns, you find there's no spare flask of oil in your kit — only what the lanterns themselves hold. That will serve: Olin's lantern is nearly full and burning clean, and the temple's hooded lantern has hours left in it yet. You leave them be.

At last it is done. The barricade stands empty behind you now, just timber and stone and the smell of spent wax, and beyond the two of you the low drift runs back toward the bend and the main drift beyond it, dark as a throat. Ged has Olin's lantern in his hand, and he looks at you, waiting for the word to move.


>>>>>> round 15, the player: "Stay close, Ged, and keep that lantern high. I'll take the lead with my shield up, and if anything shows a light ahead of us, you stop and say nothing until I tell you." I step out of the low drift toward the bend, moving slowly so he can keep pace.

