You step out first, shield raised and sword low, and Ged falls in close behind you, Olin's lantern held high so the two pools of light — yours and his — run together and throw back the dark. You move slowly, and Ged keeps pace, his breath loud in the stillness, the pick scraping once on the stone.

Around the bend, out of the low drift, and then you are back in the main drift, at the place where the fresh chalk arrow points down the way you've just come. The rails run away from you both ways: up toward the first gallery and the air, and down into the deeper dark.

You stand still a moment and listen, your eyes on the dark up the drift, the way home. And there, far ahead — low to the floor, the height a man would hold a lamp — a faint pale-yellow light sways. It is very far, beyond the reach of your own light, a small soft point in the black. It does not come toward you. It only hangs there, swinging a little, as though something stands between you and the way out and waits.

Ged sees it too. He stops dead, and his knuckles are white on Olin's lantern. He says nothing, just as you told him, but his eyes are fixed on that distant light, and his face is the colour of chalk.

The light is between you and the slope up to the first gallery. It has not moved to meet you.


