Ged answers without taking his eyes off the light, his whisper barely there: "I can't swear it's Bram, not from here. But the dead all carry lamps, and they sway like that — slow, like a man who's forgotten how to stand. A live man's light bobs and hurries. That one's waiting."

You hold up your hand and let the moments pass. The light does not come to you, and it does not fade; it only swings, patient as a heartbeat.

Then you start forward, slow, shield up, and Ged follows a few paces back with every lamp of his burning bright. The rails slide by under your boots, and the light ahead grows by degrees. Your darkvision reaches into the dark and finds it, and at last you can see what waits there.

It is Bram. You know him by his heavy boots and his grey knitted scarf and the stoop of his shoulders, though the man who wore them is gone. He stands in the middle of the drift, facing you, a lantern hanging from one slack hand and swaying with the slow roll of his body, his head canted as though listening. His eyes are open but unseeing, filmed like Olin's were. And at his collar the grey livid stain shows over his heart, the same mark that was on Olin.

But it is the lantern that holds you. Its wick is fresh-trimmed and burning clean, bright and steady, the way a careful hand trims a lamp — and you saw Olin's burn just so, lit only hours ago, though Olin had been dead for days. Bram's other hand hangs empty. The dead do not trim their own lamps.

Something in the dark lights them, and keeps them lit.

Bram has not moved to meet you. He stands between you and the slope up to the first gallery, his lantern swaying, and he waits.


