You call out to Bram, your voice low in the stone, asking for any sign that some part of him remains. The figure gives none. Its filmed eyes do not focus, its slack jaw does not move. It only takes one shambling step toward you, the lantern swaying, and then another — and it lunges.

You meet it with your shield and your sword already up. Your longsword bites across its chest, a deep slash through coat and flesh, and the thing staggers but does not fall. Its own swing comes in wide and clumsy, and you turn it off your shield with a clang that rings down the drift. Ged, behind you, holds his breath and keeps every lamp high.

The dead thing straightens again, unbothered by the wound, its lantern still burning in that slack hand. It is your turn to act, the drift narrow and the way out behind it.

What do you do?


