You step in behind your shield, the blade already singing, and take Bram across the neck. The blow nearly parts his head from his shoulders, and this time there is no strength in the body to hold. He drops where he stood, the lantern spilling from his hand to roll against the rail, its flame guttering and then steadying. The thing that wore Bram Marrow's face is gone, and the man lies still at last, at rest like Olin before him.

The drift falls quiet. Behind you, Ged lets out a long, shuddering breath. "Good," he whispers. "Good."

You look around the drift, sword still up, for whoever trims and lights these lamps. But there is no one here — only the two of you, and Bram's fallen lantern burning on the stone. You stoop and turn it. The wick is fresh-trimmed and burning clean, the oil full, just as Olin's was, and the flame is steady and patient, the work of a careful hand.

There is no hand here to have done it. The lamps of the dead are lit from somewhere deeper, in the dark below.

The way up the drift stands open now, the rails running toward the first gallery and the slope to the air, and no light waits between you and it that you can see.
